<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Augsburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1276 CE – 1806 CE · capital Augsburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Swabian imperial city of the Fugger and Welser bankers, where the Augsburg Confession was read in 1530 and the Peace of Augsburg made in 1555.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> grew from the Roman provincial capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusta-vindelicorum">Augusta Vindelicorum</a> and was ruled for centuries by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. Its citizens won increasing self-government, and in 1276 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">King Rudolf I</a> confirmed their town law, making Augsburg an imperial city independent of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-augsburg">Prince-Bishopric of Augsburg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of patricians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> governed after the guild revolution of 1368. Textile production and banking made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe's</a> richest cities; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fuggers</a> financed emperors and founded the Fuggerei social housing in 1516. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a> was presented at the diet of 1530, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> of 1555 recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>. After 1648 Catholics and Protestants shared offices equally.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> lost its freedom in 1806 when it was annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>. It remains a major Bavarian city, and the Peace Festival of 8 August, held since 1650, is still a local holiday.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Augsburg" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: Guido Radig · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augsburg_-_Markt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1276 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Augsburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Augsburg</dd></dl></aside></div>
